  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Natural Resource Mgmt &amp; Tribal</narrative>
  </title>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">&quot; The primary objective of this pilot project is to explore innovative approaches to improve the livelihoods of poor forest dependant communities by strengthening their access to and control over natural resources including state-owned forests. The expected outcomes include 1) increased capacity of  traditional community and Panchayat institutions to exercise their rights and responsibilities in forest management and decentralized natural resources planning; 2) improved coordination among service providers (state forest department, tribal institutions/local panchayats, NGOs, other line agencies); 3) stakeholders explore new roles, skills, and incentives to support, regulate, and monitor participatory natural resources management ; 4) new opportunities to access and market forest and non-forest products are identified; and 5) expanded forest resources provide increasing returns (usufruct to communities and ecosystem services to society) over time. The proposed operation is expected to cover a number of states in the &quot;&quot;Tribal belt&quot;&quot; of Central India (which states and how many will be determined through a process of dialogue with state governments in Maharashtra, Madhya Pradesh, Chhattisgarh, Orissa, and West Bengal). The project is expected to be composed of stand-alone state level operations and include a small centrally implemented component to address issues of forest policy development and sectoral monitoring and evaluation.&quot;</narrative>
  </description>
